The portable C in the scenario stay-left: what it is expected to show
otherwise than the original. A patch of harness/goldens/stay-left.txt, see
harness/goldens.py: a line with - is the golden's, a line with + is what is
expected in its place.
Deviation: 7, 10, 14 of src/portable_c/DEVIATIONS.md
Why: the program that has stayed in the memory is not released when the
program is left, since the portable C keeps no list of such programs, and it
is cut off from its interrupts all the same, by the extender. With it in the
memory and the program still running, Ctrl-Break, INT 1Bh, no longer goes to
it: two dashes where the original has 1B. When the program is left, the
extender writes a line of warning for each of INT 1Ch, 21h, 2Fh and 60h, which
it puts back: four lines above DOS's prompt, which is two rows further down,
and the cursor with it. The second program then prints five times two dashes,
as behind the original, so that row is not here: nobody answers it on INT 2Fh,
which is all that the row says. The four lines are two rows further up behind
it. The box of F10 names the program in its title and in its question, by a
name that is two letters shorter: the box is two cells narrower, a cell on
either side, and its shadow ends a cell further left. And the Advanced options
have no Quick execute commands: Clear keyboard buffer and what is below it in
the box stand a row further up, the cursor with them where it is on that box,
the box is a row shorter, and the two rows that its last row and the shadow
below it were on show what is behind the box, the upper one in the shadow

